title: The Red River Valley
subtitle: The White League demands a parish government's surrender.
tags: event
new-page: true
view-if: whiteL_formed = 1 and LA_gub_winner = "rep" and LA_whiteL_strength >= 40 and LA_kkk_strength + LA_whiteL_strength >= 100
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_whiteL_strength = (Q.LA_whiteL_strength || 0) + 4;
    Q.whiteL_strength += 2;
    Q.LA_freemen_rep -= 2;
    Q.LA_rural_dem += 2;
!}

= The Red River Valley

The White League has begun forcing <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> governments out of office along the Red River. In a neighboring parish, its men occupied the courthouse and compelled the local officials to resign. The takeover was orderly enough to avoid a battle. Its message was plain: the League could decide who governed, and the law would not stop it.

Now six white <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> officeholders in another parish have been arrested by armed League members. They stand accused of plotting an uprising by freedmen, a charge for which the League has offered no evidence. The men are told to sign their resignations and leave Louisiana. If they cooperate, they will be allowed to go. If they refuse, their captors make no promises.

The parish sheriff has few deputies and no reason to trust the men holding his colleagues. The area's leading <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> is away at a party convention, leaving the sheriff to decide whether to resist or bargain. He sends an urgent wire to the governor, who passes the matter to us.

[? if LA_occupation_posture >= 1 : There are [+ LA_garrison_troops +] thousand federal troops in Louisiana. ?][? if LA_occupation_posture < 1 : There are no federal troops in Louisiana. ?]

- @hold_posts: Tell the officials to refuse and hold their offices
- @negotiate_passage: Have them accept the terms, with a guarantee of safe passage

@hold_posts
go-to: posts_held if LA_garrison_troops >= 10; posts_overrun if LA_garrison_troops < 10

We order the officials to stand their ground. They were chosen under the law, and the White League has no authority to remove them. If we allow armed men to dictate who holds office, every <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> parish government becomes vulnerable to the same threat.

@negotiate_passage
go-to: passage_kept if LA_garrison_troops >= 5; passage_betrayed if LA_garrison_troops < 5

We authorize a negotiated departure. The officials will sign their resignations and leave the state under escort, with the governor responsible for securing their passage. It may spare their lives, but it also gives the League what it came for. We can promise protection only if we have the means to provide it.

@posts_held
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_whiteL_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.LA_whiteL_strength || 0) - 8);
    Q.LA_freemen_rep += 2;
    Q.LA_rural_dem += 2;
    Q.Union_League_strength += 4;
    Q.dem_relation -= 2;
!}

The League's leaders reconsider when they learn how many federal soldiers are within reach. An attack on the parish seat could bring the Army down on them, and they withdraw rather than risk a direct confrontation. The officials retain their posts, and <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> organizers regain some confidence.

The retreat is tactical. The League's men leave the parish without surrendering their weapons or facing arrest. They have learned where the government is prepared to draw a line, and they will look for a weaker place to cross it.

@posts_overrun
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_whiteL_strength = (Q.LA_whiteL_strength || 0) + 6;
    Q.whiteL_strength += 3;
    Q.LA_freemen_rep -= 5;
    Q.LA_rural_dem += 3;
    Q.LA_middle_class_rep -= 2;
    Q.Union_League_strength = Math.max(0, Q.Union_League_strength - 8);
    Q.freemen_rep -= 3;
    Q.radical_strength += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 3;
!}
go-to: aftermath

The sheriff refuses to surrender the parish government. The League advances on the seat in force, and the sheriff's deputies are quickly overwhelmed. The six officials are killed. Black <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> who had gathered to defend the courthouse are hunted through the surrounding streets and countryside.

The courthouse falls without a serious fight. No arrests follow, and the parish's surviving <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> officeholders flee or go into hiding. The League has secured the result it wanted: an elected government removed by force, with no effective punishment from the state.

@passage_kept
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_whiteL_strength = (Q.LA_whiteL_strength || 0) + 4;
    Q.LA_freemen_rep -= 3;
    Q.LA_rural_dem += 3;
    Q.LA_middle_class_dem += 1;
    Q.Union_League_strength = Math.max(0, Q.Union_League_strength - 6);
    Q.radical_dissent += 2;
    Q.dem_relation += 1;
!}

The resignations are signed. Under escort, the officials reach the river and board a boat bound north. None is killed, and the immediate crisis passes without a clash between the League and federal troops.

The League has still won. Its men have removed a parish government by threatening its officers, and they have paid no price for doing so. <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> organizers across the valley begin to wonder whether holding office is worth the danger. The next sheriff who receives such an ultimatum will know what happened here.

@passage_betrayed
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_whiteL_strength = (Q.LA_whiteL_strength || 0) + 8;
    Q.whiteL_strength += 4;
    Q.LA_freemen_rep -= 6;
    Q.LA_rural_dem += 3;
    Q.LA_middle_class_rep -= 2;
    Q.Union_League_strength = Math.max(0, Q.Union_League_strength - 10);
    Q.freemen_rep -= 3;
    Q.radical_strength += 3;
    Q.dem_relation -= 3;
!}
go-to: aftermath

The officials sign their resignations and set out under escort. The arrangement holds until the road bends away from the parish seat. The escort halts, the officials are ordered out, and gunmen open fire. The survivors are left beside the road.

Black men who accompanied the party as witnesses are hunted down over the following days. Some flee to neighboring parishes; others disappear without a trace. No arrests are made. The League has shown that its guarantees are worth nothing once its victims are beyond public view.

@aftermath

Word of the killings travels north, and the affair becomes another test of the government's willingness to defend Reconstruction. <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> demand action against the League. <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democrats**</span> in Louisiana deny that the organization ordered the murders and accuse <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republicans**</span> of inventing a conspiracy to justify military rule.

The governor asks us to reinforce the Red River parishes. The survivors are frightened, the local authorities are compromised, and the men responsible have had time to scatter. We can put soldiers in the area, seek prosecutions, or leave the investigation to officials who have already failed to act.

- @send_troops: Send troops into the Red River valley
- @demand_charges: Demand that federal marshals bring charges

@send_troops
choose-if: army_strength >= 5 and occupation >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: [? if occupation < 1 : We have no authority to station troops there. ?][? if army_strength < 5 : We have no troops to spare. ?]
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_occupation_posture = Math.max(1, Q.LA_occupation_posture || 0);
    Q.LA_garrison_troops = (Q.LA_garrison_troops || 0) + 5;
    Q.army_strength -= 5;
    Q.South_army_strength += 5;
    Q.LA_whiteL_strength = Math.max(0, (Q.LA_whiteL_strength || 0) - 6);
    Q.LA_freemen_rep += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 3;
    Q.conservative_dissent += 1;
!}

Federal reinforcements arrive within weeks. By then, the League's armed companies have dispersed into the countryside, leaving no concentration of men for the Army to confront. Patrols restore a measure of order around the parish seat, but the witnesses remain silent and the officials' killers are still at large.

The deployment protects the government from an immediate second attack. It does little to repair the damage already done. The League has learned that it can withdraw before the troops arrive and return when they leave.

@demand_charges
on-arrival: {!
    Q.LA_freemen_rep += 1;
    Q.radical_strength += 2;
    Q.dem_relation -= 2;
    Q.liberal_dissent += 1;
!}

We direct the federal marshals to identify the men responsible and bring charges wherever the evidence permits. They find frightened witnesses, missing records, and local officials unwilling to cooperate. The courthouse offers little help, and the League's sympathizers make clear that anyone who speaks may be next.

The investigation produces a report but no indictments. <span style="color: #7A1F5C;">**Radicals**</span> point to the affair as proof that <span style="color: #c00000;">**Republican**</span> officeholders cannot rely on local law to protect them. The governor renews his request for troops, while the League remains free to organize.